          "english": "Harvard cognitive neuroscientist Joshua Greene says, for most of us, lying takes work. In studies, he gave subjects a chance to deceive for monetary gain while examining their brains in a functional MRI machine, which maps blood flow to active parts of the brain. Some people told the truth instantly and instinctively. But others opted to lie, and they showed increased activity in their frontal parietal(颅腔壁的) control network, which is involved in difficult or complex thinking. This suggests that they were deciding between truth and dishonesty—and ultimately opting for the latter. For a follow-up analysis, he found that people whose neural(神经的) reward centers were more active when they won money were also more likely to be among the group of liars – suggesting that lying may have to do with the inability to resist temptation.",
          "chinese": "哈佛大学认知神经科学家约书亚·格林说，对我们大多数人而言，说谎需要付出努力。在研究中，他让受试者有机会通过欺骗来获取金钱，同时利用功能性磁共振成像仪检查他们的大脑。这种仪器能够绘制大脑活跃区域的血流情况。有些人会即时凭本能说出真话。但另一些人选择说谎，他们的额顶控制网络活动增强了，而这一网络参与困难或复杂的思维过程。这表明，他们当时正在真相与不诚实之间作出选择，并最终选择了后者。在后续分析中，他发现，那些在赢钱时神经奖赏中枢活动更加活跃的人，也更有可能属于说谎者群体，这说明说谎可能与无法抵制诱惑有关。",
